# Air, Orchestral Suite No. 3 in D major, BWV 1068 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the slow movement of a festive suite, for strings alone. A
# long singing melody over a bass that walks in steady eighths, the
# "Air on the G string" in its original key and shape. It needs a broad,
# calm line that never stops moving forward; the walking eighths are the
# pulse and should stay even.
#
# This arrangement: Mutopia's version for flute and guitar (Mike
# Blackstock, after the Bach-Gesellschaft), 4/4, D major. The flute has
# the melody (our top voice), the guitar's lower voice the walking bass
# (our bottom voice), and its upper voice the inner parts. Where the
# guitar plays chords there are more than three parts, so the converter
# drops some inner notes; melody and bass survive.
#
# Bar numbering: as the MIDI plays it, 19 bars, first note at tick 0, so
# there is no pickup. The score's Air is 18 bars, a first half of 6 and a
# second half of 12, both repeated. This MIDI plays NO repeats. Instead
# the first half's first and second endings are both played in turn:
#   MIDI 1-6    score 1-6 (bar 6 is the first ending: the A major
#               cadence, with the bass running back toward the opening)
#   MIDI 7      the second ending: the same cadence bar again
#   MIDI 8-19   score 7-18, the second half, once
# So every bar number below is the MIDI's; subtract 1 from bar 8 on for
# the score's number.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 36 (eighth = 72, the walking bass). Pinnock and the
# English Concert (Archiv) take 4:47 in one catalogue listing; with both
# repeats (144 quarters) that is about quarter = 30. A listing credited to
# Koopman gives 5:34 (about 26), but I could not confirm which recording
# it is. Romantic orchestral readings run slower still (Ruzicka 6:42),
# and I found no confirmed timings for Richter, Munchinger, Savall or
# Suzuki. I take it a little quicker than Pinnock: the chip's notes start
# loud and sink to a floor, so a long held note (the opening F sharp is
# four and a half beats) only stays alive if the line keeps moving.
#
# Structure (MIDI bars):
#   1-6    D major, the long opening F sharp, to the cadence in A (6)
#   7      the cadence again (second ending)
#   8-11   second half: through E minor to the cadence in B minor (11)
#   12-13  to a cadence in A major (13, beat 3)
#   14-15  the bass rises chromatically, F sharp to B, under climbing
#          suspensions, arriving on E minor (15)
#   16-17  A7 back to D; the melody climbs to the high C natural (17),
#          the top of the piece
#   18-19  the final cadence in D

tempo 36

voice top 0       # the melody sings on top
voice mid 2       # the guitar's inner chords stay well back
voice bottom 1    # the walking bass a step back, but clear: it is the
                  # second line that matters

dyn 1 mp          # bars 1-6: the first half, calm and singing
dyn 7 p           # bar 7: the repeated cadence bar played as an echo
dyn 8 mp          # bars 8-13: second half, out through B minor and A
dyn 14 mf         # bars 14-19: the chromatic climb in the bass, the high
                  # C at 17 and the close, the one fuller terrace

broaden 7 0.92    # the end of the first half: a breath before the second
broaden 11 0.95   # into the B minor cadence

rit 2 0.7         # bars 18-19: the final cadence settles
